我是莱斯利·约翰·塔拉斯·塞马伊，和我的同事安妮·菲什曼一起，我们负责中心的能源项目。

4
00:00:09,431 --> 00:00:13,149
And we're so glad that you're here to cap off a really big week this week.
我们非常高兴您今天能来到这里，为这一周的精彩活动画上句点。

5
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:15,744
It was national clean it energy week.
本周是全国清洁能源周。

6
00:00:15,794 --> 00:00:22,486
It was New York climate week and really the future of energy in America has never been so much in the spotlight.
这也是纽约气候周，而美国能源的未来从未如此受到瞩目。

7
00:00:22,536 --> 00:00:26,790
We're really excited to continue that conversation here today with all of you.
我们今天非常期待与在座各位继续这一重要对话。

8
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:30,930
The BPC energy program has been a driving force in federal policy for over 16 years and
两党政策中心能源项目在过去16年中一直是联邦政策的重要推动力。

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:37,572
we're really leading the way on building a strong bipartisan foundation of support for technology and innovation,
我们正积极推动建立一个强大的两党共识基础，支持技术与创新，

10
00:00:37,622 --> 00:00:43,190
for decarbonization, permitting reform, critical minerals, carbon management, so much more.
支持脱碳、许可改革、关键矿产、碳管理等众多领域。

11
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:49,630
And these are just pieces of a larger puzzle of how to ensure that all Americans have access to energy that is secure,
这些只是更大图景的一部分，即如何确保所有美国人都能获得安全、

12
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,270
reliable, affordable, and clean.
可靠、负担得起且清洁的能源。

13
00:00:52,320 --> 00:01:00,483
And so we're here today that because the growth of AI is a new puzzle piece that we didn't even know that we needed to account for until recently.
因此今天我们在这里，是因为人工智能的增长是一个我们直到最近才意识到需要纳入考量的新拼图。

14
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:01,979
So, you know, where where do we put it?
所以，你知道，我们该把它放在哪里？

15
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:03,824
Where does AI go in all of this?
人工智能在这一切中该处于什么位置？

16
00:01:03,874 --> 00:01:09,943
And so we're going to dig into these questions and more as part of a new BPC project that we're launching today on
因此，作为我们今天启动的一项新BPC项目的一部分，我们将深入探讨这些问题以及更多内容，

17
00:01:09,993 --> 00:01:21,439
AI and energy and I'm so excited to invite my colleague Tanya Das to the stage here to tell you more about it.
关于人工智能与能源，我非常激动地邀请我的同事塔尼娅·达斯上台，为大家详细介绍。

18
00:01:21,489 --> 00:01:24,670
Thank you.
谢谢。

19
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:25,854
Hello everyone.
大家好。

20
00:01:25,904 --> 00:01:26,520
Welcome.
欢迎大家。

21
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:35,390
I'm Tanya Das, a director with the energy program at BPC and we are thrilled today to launch a new project on AI and energy.
我是塔尼娅·达斯，两党政策中心能源项目负责人，今天非常激动地宣布启动一项关于人工智能与能源的新项目。

22
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,390
But I want to take a step back to reflect on how we got here.
但我想先退一步，回顾一下我们是如何走到这一步的。

23
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:47,430
Back in 2019, I was working with colleagues in Congress to develop a bill called the National AI Initiative Act.
回到2019年，我与国会的同事们合作，起草了一项名为《国家人工智能倡议法》的法案。

24
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:54,710
This was the first bill signed into law that set forth a national AI strategy across our scientific enterprise.
这是首部签署成为法律的法案，首次在我们的科研体系中确立了国家人工智能战略。

25
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:01,270
The bill contained foundational but modest investments across the federal government on AI R&D.
该法案在联邦政府的人工智能研发方面包含了基础但适度的投资。

26
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:07,030
At the time, we didn't have tools like chat GPT, Dolly, or Google AI overviews.
当时，我们还没有像 ChatGPT、Dolly 或 Google AI 概览这样的工具。

27
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:11,950
AI was very much in our lives already but in more of a quiet way.
人工智能早已存在于我们的生活中，但方式更为低调。

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,350
I had no idea how important of a topic AI would become just 5 years later.
我当时完全没想到，人工智能在五年后会变得如此重要。

29
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:24,110
Today, we have several billion-dollar bipartisan AI proposals being considered in the House and the Senate.
如今，众议院和参议院正在审议多项数十亿美元的两党人工智能提案。

30
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:31,779
Members of both sides of the aisle want to double down on using AI to advance science, security, and technology.
两党议员都希望加大对人工智能的投入，以推动科学、安全和技术发展。

31
00:02:31,829 --> 00:02:40,950
And the supercomputers at the Department of Energy are oversubscribed with users eager to try their AI algorithms to accelerate innovation.
能源部的超级计算机用户需求已远超供应能力，研究人员争相使用他们的AI算法以加速创新。

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,310
And at the center of it all is our energy system that's powering AI and our growing economy.
而这一切的核心是我们为人工智能和不断增长的经济提供动力的能源系统。

33
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:51,470
We are seeing electricity load growth for the first time in a decade and
我们首次看到电力负荷在十年来出现增长，

34
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:56,790
that's occurring at the same time that we're taking serious steps to decarbonize our economy.
而与此同时，我们正采取严肃措施推进经济的脱碳进程。

35
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:05,750
And we know AI can be a critical tool to modernize our electric grid, develop new energy technologies, and improve climate resilience.
我们知道，人工智能可以成为现代化电网、开发新能源技术以及提升气候韧性的重要工具。

36
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,950
And this is why we're launching a new project on the intersection of AI and energy.
这正是我们启动人工智能与能源交叉领域新项目的原因。

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,706
We are going to help policy makers identify how to leverage AI to improve our
我们将帮助政策制定者识别如何利用人工智能来提升我们的

38
00:03:15,756 --> 00:03:22,150
energy system while meeting the growing power needs for data centers and other emerging electricity loads.
在满足数据中心及其他新兴电力负荷不断增长的电力需求的同时，构建能源系统。

39
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:23,062
As a first step,
作为第一步，

40
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:27,958
today we're releasing a report on the pivotal role that the Department of Energy played in
今天，我们发布了一份报告，揭示能源部所发挥的关键作用。

41
00:03:28,008 --> 00:03:33,910
securing US leadership on AI and ideas for how the agency can lead in the future.
确保美国在人工智能领域的领导地位，并提出该机构未来引领方向的设想。

42
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:40,122
This is a critical moment for both our AI and energy systems and we need to make sure that we get this right.
这是我们人工智能和能源系统的关键时刻，我们必须确保做对。

43
00:03:40,172 --> 00:03:45,390
And I can't think of two better people to tell us how to do that than our featured speakers today.
我想不出比今天两位特邀演讲人更合适的人来告诉我们该怎么做。

44
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:51,328
Please join me in welcoming to the stage my fabulous boss, president and CEO of the Bipartisan Policy Center,
请和我一起欢迎我出色的老板、两党政策中心总裁兼首席执行官上台。

45
00:03:51,378 --> 00:03:53,650
Margaret Spellings and the co-founder,
玛格丽特·斯佩林斯和联合创始人，

46
00:03:53,700 --> 00:04:01,686
president and CEO of NVIDIA,
英伟达总裁兼首席执行官

47
00:04:01,736 --> 00:04:07,150
Jensen Huang.
黄仁勋

48
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:12,490
All right, thank you to my fabulous colleague Tanya for that warm welcome and introduction.
好的，感谢我出色的同事塔尼娅带来的热情欢迎和介绍。

49
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:14,008
Wow, what a great crowd.
哇，真是个很棒的观众群体。

50
00:04:14,058 --> 00:04:18,870
Thank you all for being here this morning and what a treat it is, Jensen, to be with you.
感谢大家今天早上前来，黄仁勋，能和你在一起真是莫大的荣幸。

51
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:20,990
I'm delighted to be I mean, we're thrilled that you're here.
我非常高兴，实际上我们非常激动，你能来到这里。

52
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:22,270
I was teasing with Jensen, you know,
我刚才跟黄仁勋开玩笑，你知道，

53
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:30,230
who's known for his famous black leather jacket outfit and I wanted to be in solidarity with him today and he shows up in a suit.
他以标志性的黑色皮夹克造型闻名，今天我想和他站在一起表示支持，结果他穿了西装来了。

54
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:36,430
The only one he has, by the way, but anyway, um It's the truth.
他唯一的一套西装，顺便说一句，但不管怎样，这是事实。

55
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:38,670
I'm not ashamed of it.
我一点也不觉得丢脸。

56
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,175
It's a good look.
这个造型很帅。

57
00:04:40,225 --> 00:04:41,877
So, as you know,
正如你们所知，

58
00:04:41,927 --> 00:04:45,938
we at BPC are proud of the role that we play as a place to bring people
我们两党政策中心一直以成为汇聚各方人士的平台为荣，

59
00:04:45,988 --> 00:04:52,326
together to find common ground and to drive towards solutions and there's no thing that needs more thinking,
促进彼此找到共识，推动解决问题，而没有任何议题比人工智能和能源更需要深入思考，

60
00:04:52,376 --> 00:04:57,630
good thinking around that than AI and energy and so we're thrilled to welcome you here today.
因此我们非常高兴今天能欢迎你们来到这里。

61
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,041
Thank you, Margaret.
谢谢你，玛格丽特。

62
00:04:59,091 --> 00:05:03,808
And so, as everybody in the room already knows, but maybe out in TV land they don't,
因此，房间里的每个人都知道，但可能电视观众还不知道，

63
00:05:03,858 --> 00:05:11,030
Jensen is the founder and of NVIDIA, a $30 company with about 30,000 employees.
黄仁勋是英伟达的创始人，一家市值300亿美元、拥有约3万名员工的公司。

64
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,415
Not quite 30 yet.
还不到30。

65
00:05:12,465 --> 00:05:13,492
Not quite 30?
还不到30？

66
00:05:13,542 --> 00:05:15,870
That's what I'm I'm sorry, three.
我是说，抱歉，是三。

67
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:17,163
You know, what's a zero?
你知道，零是什么吗？

68
00:05:17,213 --> 00:05:21,630
Well, a zero is a lot in computers but anyway, we'll get into that.
嗯，在计算机里，零可代表很多东西，不过我们稍后再深入讨论。

69
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,310
Anyway, Jensen We're we're working on Yeah, welcome, welcome.
无论如何，黄仁勋，我们正在努力，是的，欢迎，欢迎。

70
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,470
Sorry, I just I'm so impressed with you.
抱歉，我只是太佩服你了。

71
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,071
Anyway, why don't you tell us about how you got started?
无论如何，你跟我们说说你是怎么开始的？

72
00:05:31,121 --> 00:05:38,270
Give us your kind of so-called origin story and how it is that you came to be sitting with me here today.
给我们讲讲你所谓的起源故事，以及你是如何坐在这里和我面对面的。

73
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,750
My gosh.
我的天啊。

74
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,670
From where from zero?
从零开始，从哪里开始？

75
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:48,069
No, from you know, what the tell us the story of NVIDIA, NVIDIA and you and Uh we were in Silicon Valley,
不，是你们知道的，关于英伟达的故事，英伟达和你，还有我们在硅谷，

76
00:05:48,119 --> 00:05:54,470
engineers in Silicon Valley designing computers and we observed that there was a better way of designing computers.
那里有工程师在设计计算机，我们观察到设计计算机有更好的方式。

77
00:05:54,520 --> 00:06:00,910
Uh if you if you um the current computer that we use, it's called general purpose computers.
嗯，如果你看看我们目前使用的计算机，它被称为通用计算机。

78
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:06,182
It was invented the year after my birth, 1964 by IBM.
它诞生于我出生后的第二年，1964年，由IBM发明。

79
00:06:06,232 --> 00:06:08,230
It's called the IBM System 360.
它被称为IBM System 360。

80
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:08,699
Mhm.
嗯嗯。

81
00:06:08,749 --> 00:06:14,468
And it described a central processing unit, CPU, uh multitasking,
它描述了一个中央处理器，CPU，多任务处理，

82
00:06:14,518 --> 00:06:23,350
separation of operating system separation of hardware and software by this layer called operating system, IO subsystems, things like that.
操作系统将硬件和软件分离，通过一个称为操作系统的层级实现，还有I/O子系统等。

83
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:27,160
And those basic technologies are used today.
这些基本技术至今仍在使用。

84
00:06:27,210 --> 00:06:31,270
It's a lot better, a lot faster, but the basic architecture of it is the same.
它快得多，好得多，但其基本架构仍然相同。

85
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:33,904
It's a general purpose computer that can do anything.
这是一台通用计算机，可以完成任何任务。

86
00:06:33,954 --> 00:06:36,648
Now, we observed 30 years ago, that was 1993,
现在，我们30年前就观察到，那是1993年，

87
00:06:36,698 --> 00:06:44,828
we observed that that there are problems that are quite specialized where a general purpose approach is not necessarily the best.
我们发现有些问题非常特殊，通用方法并不一定是最佳选择。

88
00:06:44,878 --> 00:06:49,950
You know, and and it's no different than if you have generalists do something.
你知道，这和让通才做某件事没什么不同。

89
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,692
And it turns out that software is kind of the same way and there's
结果发现，软件也是类似的情况，存在

90
00:06:54,742 --> 00:07:00,950
a field of software called simulations and physics simulations and data processing and computer graphics.
一个叫做模拟、物理模拟、数据处理和计算机图形学的软件领域。

91
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:10,710
These problems, image processing, these problems have algorithms inside that are very computationally intensive.
这些问题，比如图像处理，其内部算法非常计算密集。

92
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:17,630
And if we could take that and run it on a specialized processor, on a specialized computer,
如果我们能把这些算法搬到专用处理器或专用计算机上运行，

93
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:25,640
we could add a chip to the computer that makes it go 100 times faster.
我们就可以在计算机中增加一块芯片，使其速度提升100倍。

94
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:30,667
How is it possible to add one chip to another chip and all of a sudden it's 100 times faster?
一块芯片如何加到另一块芯片上，突然就快了100倍？

95
00:07:30,717 --> 00:07:37,790
Well, the reason for that is of course each chip can focus each computer can focus on what it does best.
原因在于，每块芯片都能专注于自己最擅长的事情。

96
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:42,470
And we offload and accelerate this thing and we call it accelerated computing.
我们将这部分任务卸载并加速，这就叫加速计算。

97
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:51,390
Now, uh the amazing part of it of course is that the domains that we've been able to use it for has been expanding over time.
现在，当然最令人惊叹的是，我们能够应用它的领域随着时间推移不断扩展。

98
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:58,410
And uh it's not it's very routine that we'll accelerate an application 10, 20, 50 times.
而且我们让应用程序加速10倍、20倍甚至50倍，这已经非常常规。

99
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:05,562
Um and when you do that, even though you accelerate it by 50 times, since you only added one more chip,
当你做到这一点时，即使只多加了一颗芯片，就能实现50倍的加速，

100
00:08:05,612 --> 00:08:14,316
you reduce the amount of power by 25 25 times, the amount of energy by 25 times, the amount by 25 times.
功耗降低25倍，能耗降低25倍，成本也降低25倍。

101
00:08:14,366 --> 00:08:20,916
And so so that this this approach has led to um solving all kinds of interesting problems.
因此，这种方法已经帮助我们解决了各种各样的有趣问题。

102
00:08:20,966 --> 00:08:27,940
The first application we used it for in scientific in in in commercial use was for video games.
我们首次在科学和商业领域应用它，是用于电子游戏。

103
00:08:27,990 --> 00:08:28,590
Mhm.
嗯。

104
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:32,710
You know, for a lot of people, NVIDIA is still the video game company.
对很多人来说，英伟达仍然是游戏公司。

105
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:36,422
We build we build more computers for video games than than any company in the world.
我们为电子游戏制造的计算机，比世界上任何公司都多。

106
00:08:36,472 --> 00:08:40,547
When you have when you play PC games, it's probably NVIDIA inside.
当你玩PC游戏时，里面很可能就是英伟达的芯片。

107
00:08:40,597 --> 00:08:42,754
When you play the Nintendo, it's NVIDIA inside.
当你玩任天堂游戏时，里面也是英伟达的芯片。

108
00:08:42,804 --> 00:08:45,103
And so so we're well known for that.
因此，我们在这一领域非常出名。

109
00:08:45,153 --> 00:08:52,672
Um but some of our industrial applications, the first one was molecular dynamic simulation for virtual screening, Mhm.
但我们的某些工业应用中，第一个是用于虚拟筛选的分子动力学模拟，嗯。

110
00:08:52,722 --> 00:08:56,201
uh seismic processing for energy discovery.
用于能源勘探的地震数据处理。

111
00:08:56,251 --> 00:09:06,166
And then, you know, fluid dynamics, weather simulation, and then one day artificial intelligence found us and and so um accelerated computing,
然后是流体动力学、天气模拟，直到某一天人工智能找到了我们，于是加速计算应运而生。

112
00:09:06,216 --> 00:09:10,516
that was was an observation about the future computing and it turned out to be right.
这是对未来计算的一种观察，结果证明是正确的。

113
00:09:10,566 --> 00:09:18,190
And so when you extend that into what you call sustainable computing, this is where you get into this savings of energy and being more efficient.
因此当你将其延伸到所谓的可持续计算时，就进入了节能和效率提升的领域。

114
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:25,970
that we made is is that a general purpose approach to solving every problem is energy inefficient.
我们发现，用通用方法解决所有问题的方式是能源效率低下的。

115
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:26,470
Mhm.
嗯。

116
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:28,230
You're inefficiently doing something.
你在低效地做某件事。

117
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:29,798
It's a little bit like this.
这有点像这样。

118
00:09:29,848 --> 00:09:34,950
Suppose suppose you made a factory for building a car.
